  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 104 PM CDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nFriday through Wednesday:\n\nThe upper-level trough continues to migrate its way through the\nGreat Lakes on Friday. Given the slower departure of the trough\nand wrap around showers on the backside of the departing \nsurface low, cannot rule out some light precip lingering through\nday break Friday morning. Otherwise, Friday is trending more \nfall like with blustery, northerly winds and colder temps. \nDaytime highs only looking to top off in the 50s. However, as \nwinds decouple and high builds to our west, overnight lows are \nlooking to dip toward freezing into Saturday morning. While we \nmay not be a fan of the colder temps, this is not unexpected and\nis typical for this time of year for southern WI as the average\nfirst 32F day for fall is around 10/21 for MKE and around 10/11\nfor MSN. Thus frost/freeze headlines are favored at this time \nfor at least Friday night into early Saturday morning with \nslightly warmer and more frosty conditions for Sunday.\n\nOtherwise, the rest of the weekend is trending drier as upper-\nlevel ridge builds across the region as the surface high \npressure works its way east across the Midwest. Temps look to \nremain near normal through the day Saturday, but as the high \nslides further east and southerly winds and WAA increases, temps\nare progged to gradually warm each day back to above normal \nSunday through the start of next week.\n\nWagner",
